  "name": "ADRENALINE (EPINEPHRINE) 24HR URINE",
  "code": "1014",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"175\" data-end=\"496\">The <strong data-start=\"179\" data-end=\"223\">24-hour urinary adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> test measures the total amount of <strong data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"273\">epinephrine</strong> excreted in the urine over a day. Epinephrine is a <strong data-start=\"325\" data-end=\"342\">catecholamine</strong> produced by the <strong data-start=\"359\" data-end=\"378\">adrenal medulla</strong> and plays a vital role in the <strong data-start=\"409\" data-end=\"439\">&ldquo;fight or flight&rdquo; response</strong>, influencing heart rate, blood pressure, and metabolism.</p>\n<p data-start=\"498\" data-end=\"589\">This test is primarily used to help <strong data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"577\">diagnose catecholamine-secreting tumors</strong>, such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"590\" data-end=\"703\">\n<li data-start=\"590\" data-end=\"637\">\n<p data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"637\"><strong data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"612\">Pheochromocytoma</strong> (tumor of adrenal gland)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"638\" data-end=\"703\">\n<p data-start=\"640\" data-end=\"703\"><strong data-start=\"640\" data-end=\"657\">Paraganglioma</strong> (extra-adrenal catecholamine-secreting tumor)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"705\" data-end=\"880\">It may also be used to investigate causes of <strong data-start=\"750\" data-end=\"778\">unexplained hypertension</strong>, <strong data-start=\"780\" data-end=\"808\">episodes of palpitations</strong>, <strong data-start=\"810\" data-end=\"821\">anxiety</strong>, <strong data-start=\"823\" data-end=\"835\">sweating</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"840\" data-end=\"853\">headaches</strong>, especially when episodic.</p>\n<p data-start=\"936\" data-end=\"986\">High levels of urinary epinephrine may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"987\" data-end=\"1382\">\n<li data-start=\"987\" data-end=\"1085\">\n<p data-start=\"989\" data-end=\"1085\"><strong data-start=\"989\" data-end=\"1009\">Pheochromocytoma</strong> &ndash; often shows significantly elevated catecholamines and their metabolites</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1086\" data-end=\"1176\">\n<p data-start=\"1088\" data-end=\"1176\"><strong data-start=\"1088\" data-end=\"1105\">Paraganglioma</strong> &ndash; similar presentation, though may be located outside adrenal glands</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1177\" data-end=\"1213\">\n<p data-start=\"1179\" data-end=\"1213\"><strong data-start=\"1179\" data-end=\"1213\">Acute stress or severe anxiety</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1214\" data-end=\"1234\">\n<p data-start=\"1216\" data-end=\"1234\"><strong data-start=\"1216\" data-end=\"1232\">Hypoglycemia</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1235\" data-end=\"1294\">\n<p data-start=\"1237\" data-end=\"1294\"><strong data-start=\"1237\" data-end=\"1266\">Intense physical exercise</strong>, trauma, or acute illness</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1295\" data-end=\"1382\">\n<p data-start=\"1297\" data-end=\"1382\"><strong data-start=\"1297\" data-end=\"1320\">Certain medications</strong> (e.g., MAO inhibitors, levodopa, tricyclic antidepressants)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1384\" data-end=\"1586\">In the right clinical setting, persistently elevated levels support the diagnosis of <strong data-start=\"1469\" data-end=\"1503\">catecholamine-producing tumors</strong>. Further confirmation is often done with plasma metanephrines and imaging studies.</p>",
